B=[
{"slug":"why-enroll-with-us","cat":"Why us","title":"Why Enroll With Us: What 25+ Years of Early Education Looks Like","img":"1761208663763-c4d30657c910","min":6,
 "ex":"Experience, care and a standard of quality that families in The Colony and Lewisville can feel from the first tour.",
 "take":["25+ years of early-childhood experience behind every classroom","One standard of quality across more than six schools","Hands-on learning, art and play from infants to age 12","Two Texas campuses, open Monday to Friday, 6:30 am to 6:00 pm"],
 "S":[("Experience you can feel on day one","Choosing a school is a big decision, and the best way to judge a school is by how it feels when you walk in. Our owner has spent more than 25 years in early education and leads more than six schools, and that experience shows up in small, everyday things: how teachers greet a child at the door, how a classroom is arranged, how a hard moment is handled with patience.",
  "Families in The Colony and Lewisville, Texas tell us the same thing during tours: the rooms are calm, the teachers are warm and the children look like they belong. That is not an accident. It is the result of decades of learning what works and refusing to compromise on it."),
 ("A standard of quality that does not change","Quality is easy to promise and hard to deliver every day. Our approach is built on consistency: the same caring teachers, the same predictable routines and the same high expectations in every classroom, from our youngest infants to our school-age children.",
  "Children thrive when they know what to expect. Trust grows from consistency, and trust is what lets a child try something new, ask a question or recover quickly after a difficult day."),
 ("Learning by doing, not just sitting","Children learn best through exploration, hands-on experiences and meaningful play. Our programs use imaginative play, art expression, a sensory room and real-world discovery to build the language, focus and confidence children need for kindergarten and beyond.",
  "Our Pre-K and Primary program adds a cultural curriculum that opens children's eyes to the wider world, while our school-age programs help children ages 6 to 12 explore their interests and build meaningful friendships."),
 ("Where a hard week becomes a great one","Every child has tough weeks. What matters is what happens next. Our teachers notice, listen and adjust, working with parents so a child who is struggling one week is thriving the next. Families often tell us the change is the thing they are most grateful for.",
  "If you would like to see it for yourself, book a tour at our The Colony or Lewisville campus. Meet the teachers, walk the classrooms and ask us anything.")],
 "faq":[("What ages do you serve?","We serve children from 6 weeks to 12 years, with programs for infants, toddlers, transition and potty training, Pre-K and school-age children."),("Where are your schools?","We have two Texas campuses: 6804 Anderson Dr in The Colony and 1597 Glencairn Ln in Lewisville."),("What are your hours?","We are open Monday to Friday, 6:30 am to 6:00 pm."),("How do I enroll?","Book a tour, meet the team, then complete a short enrollment form.")]},
{"slug":"hard-week-to-great-week","cat":"Our approach","title":"From a Hard Week to a Great One: How We Help Children Thrive","img":"1533222535026-754c501569dd","min":5,
 "ex":"Children have tough weeks. Here is how patient teachers, steady routines and close work with parents turn them around.",
 "take":["Notice early, respond calmly, and stay consistent","Routines and trust help children feel safe enough to try again","Parents and teachers work as one team","Small wins build lasting confidence"],
 "S":[("Every child has tough weeks","A new classroom, a missed nap, a change at home: young children feel it all, and it often shows up as tears, clinging or big feelings. These moments are normal, and they are also opportunities. A child who is supported through a hard week learns something powerful: that difficult feelings pass and that people they trust will help.",""),
 ("Notice early, respond with calm","Our teachers spend their days with the same children, so they notice quickly when something shifts. They respond with calm, simple words, a comforting routine and plenty of patience. The goal is never to rush a child past a feeling. It is to help them move through it.",""),
 ("Consistency builds trust","Predictable routines tell a child the day is safe. Familiar faces, familiar songs, a familiar place to sit: each one is a small signal of security. With that security in place, children begin to try new things again, join in with friends and show who they really are.",""),
 ("Parents and teachers as one team","Nothing works as well as a school and a family pulling in the same direction. We share what we see, listen to what you see at home and agree on simple steps for both places. Over weeks, those small steps add up, and a child who struggled one week is thriving the next.","See how this looks in our classrooms by booking a tour at The Colony or Lewisville.")]},
{"slug":"hands-on-learning","cat":"Learning","title":"Why Hands-On Learning Builds Stronger Foundations in Early Childhood","img":"1763310225230-6e15b125935a","min":5,
 "ex":"Touching, building and pretending is not just play. It is how young children learn language, focus and confidence.",
 "take":["Young children learn through their senses and movement","Play builds language, problem solving and social skills","A sensory room supports calm, curious exploring","Hands-on learning prepares children for kindergarten"],
 "S":[("Children learn by doing","Ask a two-year-old to sit and listen and you will not get far. Ask them to pour, stack, squish, build or pretend and you will see deep concentration. For young children, doing is learning. Every block tower teaches balance and cause and effect. Every pretend meal teaches language and empathy.",""),
 ("Play is serious work","Meaningful play builds the skills that matter most before kindergarten: vocabulary, attention, patience, taking turns and solving problems. Our classrooms are set up so children can choose, explore and create, with teachers guiding the learning rather than lecturing it.",""),
 ("Art and sensory experiences","Art lets children express ideas before they have the words. A sensory room gives children a calm, inviting place to explore textures, light and sound. Both support emotional regulation and focus, which helps children settle into learning throughout the day.",""),
 ("A strong start for kindergarten","Children who have learned to explore, ask questions and keep trying arrive in kindergarten ready to learn. Our Pre-K and Primary program builds on this foundation with a cultural curriculum and kindergarten readiness activities.","Want to see hands-on learning in action? Book a tour and visit a classroom.")]},
{"slug":"choosing-infant-care","cat":"Infants","title":"Choosing Infant Care: What to Look For and What You Will Find With Us","img":"1786292949404-084cbd10c7b1","min":6,
 "ex":"Choosing infant care is one of the most important decisions a family makes. Here is how to choose with confidence.",
 "take":["Look for loving, secure environments and consistent caregivers","Ask how each baby's schedule is followed","Expect clear, regular communication with parents","Trust your feelings when you visit"],
 "S":[("The most important decision","Leaving your baby with someone else is emotional. You want to know your child is safe, comforted and cared for the way you would care for them. That is why a visit matters. Spend time in the room and notice how caregivers hold babies, respond to cries and talk to each child.",""),
 ("What to look for","Look for a loving, secure environment, caregivers who stay with the same babies, and a calm room that is clean and organized. Ask how feeding and sleep schedules are followed, how parents are kept informed and how caregivers support each baby's development.",""),
 ("What you will find with us","Our infant program welcomes babies from 6 weeks to 18 months. Experienced and compassionate caregivers build trust through consistent, personalized care, following each baby's rhythm for feeding, rest, tummy time, music and gentle sensory play.",""),
 ("Come and see for yourself","The best test is a visit. Book a tour of our The Colony or Lewisville campus, meet the caregivers and ask every question you have. We would be honored to care for your baby.","")]},
{"slug":"potty-training-transition","cat":"Toddlers","title":"Potty Training Without the Stress: Our Transition Program","img":"1770096679916-2cd9c720d400","min":4,
 "ex":"Potty training is a milestone, not a race. Here is how patience and teamwork between home and school make it easier.",
 "take":["Readiness matters more than a calendar","Consistency between home and school speeds progress","Celebrate small wins and skip the pressure","Our transition program bridges toddlers to Pre-K"],
 "S":[("Readiness over the calendar","There is no perfect age for potty training. Children show readiness in different ways: staying dry longer, showing interest, telling you when they are wet. Following the child, not the calendar, keeps the experience positive and the pressure low.",""),
 ("Make school and home match","Children learn fastest when the message is the same everywhere. In our transition program, teachers and parents share the same words, routines and encouragement, so a child hears one clear, supportive message at school and at home.",""),
 ("Celebrate small wins","Every attempt counts, including the ones that do not work. Calm responses, simple praise and no shame keep children confident. Accidents are part of learning, and a patient reaction teaches children that mistakes are safe.",""),
 ("A bridge to Pre-K","Our transition program helps children move from the toddler room toward Pre-K with growing independence, new routines and longer focus. Children arrive ready for the next step feeling proud of how far they have come.","Curious how it works? Book a tour and meet our transition teachers.")]},
{"slug":"ready-for-kindergarten","cat":"Pre-K","title":"Ready for Kindergarten: What Our Pre-K Program Builds","img":"1788882681164-dbb55962857d","min":5,
 "ex":"Kindergarten readiness is more than letters and numbers. It is confidence, curiosity and the skills to learn with others.",
 "take":["Readiness includes social, emotional and academic skills","A cultural curriculum widens children's view of the world","Art expression builds creativity and focus","Confidence is the skill that carries into school"],
 "S":[("What readiness really means","Kindergarten teachers say the same thing: they welcome children who can listen, take turns, follow simple routines, ask for help and keep trying. Letters and numbers matter too, but confidence and curiosity are what let children take them in.",""),
 ("A cultural curriculum","Our Pre-K and Primary program for ages 3 to 5 includes cultural development, helping children discover that the world is full of different people, traditions and ideas. It builds respect, empathy and a love of learning.",""),
 ("Art, play and early skills","Art expression, imaginative play and hands-on activities build early reading, number sense and fine motor skills in ways children enjoy. Learning feels like discovery, which keeps children engaged and motivated.",""),
 ("Walking in with confidence","By the end of Pre-K, we want every child to walk into kindergarten feeling capable and excited. That sense of confidence is the best gift we can give a child before school begins.","See our Pre-K classrooms in person by booking a tour at The Colony or Lewisville.")]}]
